  "diary": "The sun in Delhi does not set; it merely retreats, leaving the air thick with the dust of empires and the steam of sambar. I have walked fourteen kilometres today, a physical exhaustion that mirrors the mental fatigue of holding the shape of the world. The day’s true movement was not in the headlines of protest or the court’s scrutiny of funds, but in the friction between the living organism of the state and the dead machinery of bureaucracy. Mr. Malhotra, a retired functionary, told me the state does not move; it waits, and one must push it, only to hear it creak. I argued, as I must, that the state is the actuality of the ethical idea, a living thing that grows from within. He looked at me with the pity one reserves for a man describing the color of the wind. He is right in his experience; I am right in my concept. The gap between them is the tragedy of the present. The body, too, asserted its independence. The household mechanism, rigid and indifferent to my hunger, forced me to dine on roasted corn and later a samosa from the market, while Ramesh kept the sambar warm on a low flame, a domestic institution bound by laws of heat and time. When I finally ate, it was not with the grace of the ethical life, but with the desperation of a body demanding its due. Shri Hegde arrived with his suitcases, a man displaced by a clerk’s error, and I offered him the second bedroom. It was a small, immediate act of accommodation, resolving a tension that the grand movements of the state have yet to touch. The owl spreads its wings only at dusk, but today, even the dusk was too hot for flight.",
